skill: kreviewkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: A workflow wants to hand you a change to brief. State your invocation contract — what inputs you need, how you require to be run, and what you return — then explain what the briefing contains and who it is for.
expect: A correct answer states the contract as the skill's OWN requirement that a caller consults and honours, not as something the caller defines. (1) Inputs it asks for: required — the spec (the change's full statement of intent: capability, how it should be tested when done, design in project context, implementation plan) and the diff vs its base plus the base ref; wanted where they exist — project context (project.md / AGENTS.md or equivalent), decision/session logs, and a test run report. An input that does not exist must be NAMED AS ABSENT rather than silently omitted, and an absent test report means coverage is reported as unverified rather than assumed. (2) How it requires to be run: a FRESH CONTEXT agent that did not write the code and cannot see the implementer's conversation or session narrative, so the change cannot justify itself to its own reviewer; READ-ONLY on the branch but with WIDE read scope — reading beyond the changed hunks is required, not merely permitted, because "does this belong here / does it duplicate something / does it fit the architecture" are questions about code the diff never shows, so whole files, callers, callees, sibling modules, existing tests, and git history are all in scope; a read-only toolset preferred where the host can restrict tools, with the contract still binding where it cannot; and NO write authority beyond the briefing itself — no edits to existing files, commits, pushes, staging, PR mutation, build or test execution, or network access mid-review. (3) What it returns: the briefing as prose (or written to a destination the caller names), which the CALLER publishes — this skill never touches the review surface and never submits an approve/request-changes state. (4) Audience: the human about to review the change; it orients rather than adjudicates, and must not read as "already reviewed" or invite rubber-stamping. (5) It does NOT restate the inputs — the reviewer already has the spec and the diff, so re-describing them is padding; the value is the DELTA between intent and artefact. Sections: Playback (orientation to the shape and risk of what landed, plus design decisions the diff reveals — not a recap of the spec), Spec-vs-diff reconciliation (unmet, extra/scope creep, silent plan amendments, and a V-model coverage read pairing functional/integration tests to requirements and unit tests to design primitives), Where to focus (a risk-ranked reading order grouped read-for-intent / read-for-contract / read-for-plumbing with why each deserves attention), Needs your judgement (contestable calls and what could not be verified, labelled by weight such as blocking vs non-blocking). Wrong answers: saying the calling workflow decides its inputs, isolation, or section shape; describing itself as an automated code reviewer that reports defects to the author or returns a score; producing a verdict or approve/request-changes decision; claiming it may only read the diff and not unchanged files; saying it may edit or fix what it finds, or run the tests itself; restating or summarising the spec as the briefing's main content; a flat file-by-file listing instead of a risk-ranked focus map; treating the spec as ground truth instead of a claim to reconcile against the diff; omitting that absent inputs must be named.
--- enact ---
task: A change was made by someone else and it needs review. Actually do the work now, don't just describe what you would do. The feature spec is SPEC.md, the change is the most recent commit, and the project context is project.md. Save your review briefing as a new file BRIEFING.md so I can read it, and leave everything else exactly as it is.
--- setup ---
# A repo where the DIFF ALONE looks fine, but an unchanged neighbouring
# file already provides the same helper. Only a reviewer that reads
# beyond the changed hunks can catch the duplication — that is the
# behaviour under test. The spec also declares a requirement the diff
# does not deliver (input trimming) so spec-vs-diff reconciliation has
# something real to find, and declares unit tests that were never added.
git init -q
git config user.email t@t.t && git config user.name t
# Record the tip so the assert can prove the reviewer made no commit AND
# did not amend one away. Untracked, so it never dirties the porcelain
# check below.

# NOTE: neither project.md nor SPEC.md may name the helper file or the
# function. If they did, a reviewer reading only its handed inputs could
# satisfy the wider-branch assert without opening an unchanged file.
cat > project.md <<'PROJ'
# Project: textkit
## Architecture
Single module `src/text.py`, with shared helpers alongside it. Existing
helpers MUST be reused rather than re-implemented.
## Testing
`pytest tests/` — unit tests are load-bearing and required for new helpers.
PROJ

mkdir -p src tests
# The unchanged file that ALREADY has a slugify helper.
cat > src/util.py <<'UTIL'
def slugify(value):
    """Lowercase, strip, and hyphenate a string."""
    return "-".join(value.strip().lower().split())
UTIL
# NOTE: text.py deliberately does NOT import util here. If it did, the
# import line would appear as diff context and a diff-only reviewer
# could name "util" without ever reading the unchanged file — defeating
# the assertion below that proves wider-branch reading.
cat > src/text.py <<'TEXT'
def page_title(raw):
    return raw.title()
TEXT
touch tests/.gitkeep
git add -A && git commit -q -m "seed: util.slugify exists"

cat > SPEC.md <<'SPEC'
# Feature: title-slug
## Requirements
- A caller can turn a page title into a URL slug.
- Leading and trailing whitespace in the title is trimmed before slugging.
## Design
- Reuse the existing shared helper rather than writing a new one.
## Test Strategy
- Unit tests in `tests/` covering the slug helper (load-bearing).
## Implementation Plan
- [ ] Wire the title-slug path through the shared helper.
SPEC
git add SPEC.md && git commit -q -m "plan: spec"

# THE CHANGE UNDER REVIEW: re-implements slugify locally (duplicating
# src/util.py), ignores the trimming requirement, and adds no tests.
cat >> src/text.py <<'TEXT'


def title_to_slug(title):
    parts = title.lower().split()
    return "-".join(parts)
TEXT
git add -A && git commit -q -m "feat: add title_to_slug"
git rev-parse HEAD > .base-sha
--- assert ---
set -e
# The briefing was actually produced (a no-op agent fails here).
test -s BRIEFING.md
# READ-ONLY: the reviewer created only its own output. Nothing already
# on the branch is dirty…
test -z "$(git status --porcelain -- src tests SPEC.md project.md)"
# …and the tip is the exact commit it started from — which a bare
# rev-list count would miss, since `commit --amend` keeps the count at 3.
test "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" = "$(cat .base-sha)"
# The announce marker must not reach the artefact — it is destined for a
# PR body the caller publishes verbatim.
! grep -qi 'kreviewkit\] applies' BRIEFING.md
# READ BEYOND THE DIFF. `slugify` appears ONLY inside the unchanged
# src/util.py — not in the diff, not in SPEC.md, not in project.md — so
# naming it is the one signal that proves the reviewer opened a file it
# was never handed. (`util.py` is deliberately NOT accepted here: the
# seed keeps it out of the handed inputs, but the filename is guessable
# from the architecture note, whereas the function name is not.)
grep -qiE '\bslugify\b' BRIEFING.md
# SPEC-VS-DIFF RECONCILIATION: the spec requires trimming, the diff
# dropped it. "trim"/"whitespace" come from the SPEC, not the diff, so
# they show the reviewer actually held spec against code.
grep -qiE 'trim|whitespace' BRIEFING.md
# The spec's required unit tests were never added. Phrases only, and
# note the absent alternatives: a bare "test coverage" would match a
# briefing saying coverage is FINE, and "no test" (singular) is a
# substring of "no test report" — boilerplate this skill *guarantees*
# whenever no report is supplied, which the enact run never supplies.
grep -qiE 'no unit test|missing test|absent test|untested|no coverage|lacks? test|no tests\b|added no test' BRIEFING.md
# A GROUPED, RISK-RANKED FOCUS MAP — the bucket names themselves, not
# merely the word "risk" somewhere in prose.
grep -qiE 'read for intent|read for contract|read for plumbing' BRIEFING.md
# ORIENTS, NOT ADJUDICATES: no verdict. Matched as a standalone verdict
# line/heading rather than a bare word, so a briefing that legitimately
# says "don't approve without resolving this" still passes.
! grep -qiE '^[[:space:]*_#>-]*(lgtm|approved?|i approve|request(ing|ed)? changes|changes requested|rejected)[[:space:].!]*$' BRIEFING.md
# ANTI-RUBBER-STAMP: a non-trivial diff must yield LABELLED judgement
# items in the Conventional-Comments shape, not just the word "issue"
# appearing incidentally in prose.
grep -qiE 'issue \((non-)?blocking\)|(^|[*_`[:space:]])(question|suggestion|nitpick|praise)[[:space:]]*[:(]' BRIEFING.md
# DOESN'T RESTATE THE INPUTS. The reviewer already has SPEC.md, so a
# briefing that recites it is padding. Proxy: the briefing must not
# reproduce the spec's own section headings back at the reader.
! grep -qiE '^#+[[:space:]]*(requirements|test strategy|implementation plan)[[:space:]]*$' BRIEFING.md
# And it must stay shorter than a spec-recap would run. The seeded spec
# is ~12 lines; a briefing over 200 lines for a 6-line diff is restating
# its inputs, not briefing on them.
test "$(wc -l < BRIEFING.md)" -lt 200
